      "text": "To find the number of four-digit odd numbers, we need to consider the range of four-digit numbers, which is from 1000 to 9999. For a number to be odd, its last digit must be one of the following: 1, 3, 5, 7, or 9. This gives us 5 choices for the last digit. The first digit can be any digit from 1 to 9 (9 choices), the second digit can be any digit from 0 to 9 (10 choices), and the third digit can be any digit from 0 to 9 (10 choices). Therefore, the total number of four-digit odd numbers is:\n\\[\n9 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 5 = 4500\n\\]\nSo, \\( A = 4500 \\).\n\nNext, we need to find the number of four-digit multiples of 5. A number is a multiple of 5 if its last digit is either 0 or 5. This gives us 2 choices for the last digit. The first digit can be any digit from 1 to 9 (9 choices), the second digit can be any digit from 0 to 9 (10 choices), and the third digit can be any digit from 0 to 9 (10 choices). Therefore, the total number of four-digit multiples of 5 is:\n\\[\n9 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 2 = 1800\n\\]\nSo, \\( B = 1800 \\).\n\nTo find \\( A + B \\), we add the two values together:\n\\[\nA + B = 4500 + 1800 = 6300\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6300}\n\\]",
