      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find \\( A \\), the number of four-digit odd numbers, we consider the structure of a four-digit number. A number \\( \\overline{abcd} \\) is a four-digit number if \\( 1000 \\leq \\overline{abcd} < 10000 \\). For this number to be odd, the last digit \\( d \\) must be one of \\( \\{1, 3, 5, 7, 9\\} \\).\n\n1. The first digit \\( a \\) can be one of \\( \\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9\\} \\) since \\( \\overline{abcd} \\) must be non-zero.\n2. The second and third digits \\( b \\) and \\( c \\) can be any digit from \\( 0 \\) to \\( 9 \\).\n\nThus, for each possible value of \\( a \\):\n- \\( d \\) has 5 choices.\n- \\( b \\) has 10 choices.\n- \\( c \\) has 10 choices.\n\nThe total number of four-digit odd numbers is then:\n\\[\nA = 9 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 5 = 4500\n\\]\n\nNext, to find \\( B \\), the number of four-digit multiples of 5. A number \\( \\overline{abcd} \\) is a multiple of 5 if its last digit \\( d \\) is either \\( 0 \\) or \\( 5 \\).\n\n1. When \\( d = 0 \\):\n- The number \\( \\overline{abcd} \\) must be a four-digit number, so \\( 1000 \\leq \\overline{abcd} < 10000 \\).\n- For \\( \\overline{abcd} \\) to be a multiple of 5, the last digit must be \\( 0 \\).\n- The first digit \\( a \\) can be any digit from \\( 1 \\) to \\( 9 \\) (10 choices).\n- The second and third digits \\( b \\) and \\( c \\) can be any digit from \\( 0 \\) to \\( 9 \\) (10 choices each).\n\nThus, the number of four-digit multiples of 5 starting with \\( 0 \\) is:\n\\[\n10 \\times 10 \\times 10 = 1000\n\\]\n\n2. When \\( d = 5 \\):\n- Similarly, \\( \\overline{abcd} \\) must be a four-digit number, so \\( 1000 \\leq \\overline{abcd} < 10000 \\).\n- For \\( \\overline{abcd} \\) to be a multiple of 5, the last digit must be \\( 5 \\).\n- The first digit \\( a \\) can be any digit from \\( 1 \\) to \\( 9 \\) (10 choices).\n- The second and third digits \\( b \\) and \\( c \\) can be any digit from \\( 0 \\) to \\( 9 \\) (10 choices each).\n\nThus, the number of four-digit multiples of 5 starting with \\( 5 \\) is:\n\\[\n10 \\times 10 \\times 10 = 1000\n\\]\n\nSo, the total number of four-digit multiples of 5 is:\n\\[\nB = 1000 + 1000 = 2000\n\\]\n\nFinally, the sum \\( A + B \\) is:\n\\[\nA + B = 4500 + 2000 = 6500\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6500}\n\\]",
